WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:08.160 --> 00:00:12.799
hi it's michelle from lab muffin beauty
science chemistry phd and sunscreen nerd

00:00:12.799 --> 00:00:15.679
today i'm going to be talking about one
of the questions i always get asked

00:00:15.679 --> 00:00:18.000
about sunscreen
you put it on in the morning and then

00:00:18.000 --> 00:00:21.199
you put your makeup on top and then
you're meant to reapply it every two

00:00:21.199 --> 00:00:25.439
hours

00:00:25.439 --> 00:00:28.800
so how are you meant to do this without
messing up your makeup

00:00:28.800 --> 00:00:31.840
and do you really need to reapply that
often

00:00:31.840 --> 00:00:35.760
so that's what we're talking about today
if you like nerding out about beauty

00:00:35.760 --> 00:00:39.200
you can click the thumbs up subscribe
and click the notification bell so you

00:00:39.200 --> 00:00:48.079
don't miss any videos
why do we need to reapply sunscreen if

00:00:48.079 --> 00:00:51.280
you look on your sunscreen packaging
usually on the back it'll say something

00:00:51.280 --> 00:00:54.399
like
reapply every two hours the reason is

00:00:54.399 --> 00:00:57.039
that sunscreen gets less effective over
time

00:00:57.039 --> 00:01:01.199
there are a few reasons for this firstly
sunscreen comes off if you brush against

00:01:01.199 --> 00:01:03.680
it with your hair your hands your
clothes

00:01:03.680 --> 00:01:07.840
less sunscreen means less protection the
second reason is that when you first

00:01:07.840 --> 00:01:10.720
apply sunscreen
it forms an even continuous layer on

00:01:10.720 --> 00:01:13.920
your skin but
over time this layer becomes less and

00:01:13.920 --> 00:01:16.960
less even it starts breaking up and
clumping up

00:01:16.960 --> 00:01:20.799
this is because of things like oil
coming out of your skin any sweat

00:01:20.799 --> 00:01:25.040
and also just moving your face around
this isn't sunscreen but it's foundation

00:01:25.040 --> 00:01:28.080
which is another product that's meant to
stay on your skin for as long as

00:01:28.080 --> 00:01:30.880
possible in an even layer
so it uses a lot of the same

00:01:30.880 --> 00:01:34.960
technologies you can see that it clumps
up and moves around and migrates into

00:01:34.960 --> 00:01:38.240
pores over time
you can also see this on my video on

00:01:38.240 --> 00:01:41.439
foundation clumping
the sunscreen film also gets thinner

00:01:41.439 --> 00:01:44.640
because it starts evaporating and
absorbing into your skin

00:01:44.640 --> 00:01:47.759
the last reason is the one that people
mostly talk about

00:01:47.759 --> 00:01:51.680
and that is some sunscreen filters get
less effective over time as they absorb

00:01:51.680 --> 00:01:54.799
more uv
this is called photoinstability and it

00:01:54.799 --> 00:01:58.320
only actually applies to some of the
older chemical filters

00:01:58.320 --> 00:02:01.680
sunscreens these days are usually
formulated to be photostable

00:02:01.680 --> 00:02:05.040
so they'll use some of the newer
chemical filters that are photostable

00:02:05.040 --> 00:02:08.080
or they'll use mineral filters or
they'll use special

00:02:08.080 --> 00:02:12.000
technologies that make the photo-
unstable filters stable enough that

00:02:12.000 --> 00:02:15.840
it's not really a big worry that means
that they won't have this specific

00:02:15.840 --> 00:02:18.480
problem
but the first two issues still apply so

00:02:18.480 --> 00:02:22.000
they still do get
less effective over time so at the start

00:02:22.000 --> 00:02:25.120
when you apply your sunscreen that's
when your sunscreen is most effective

00:02:25.120 --> 00:02:27.599
and then over time it gets less and less
effective

00:02:27.599 --> 00:02:31.599
but how much less are we talking about?

00:02:34.800 --> 00:02:38.720
unfortunately there's not that much info
on how long sunscreen lasts on your skin

00:02:38.720 --> 00:02:42.480
if you aren't moving around that much
i've only managed to find one study

00:02:42.480 --> 00:02:45.680
directly on this topic
20 people applied sunscreen with a

00:02:45.680 --> 00:02:48.800
special dye in it so that the
researchers could photograph them every

00:02:48.800 --> 00:02:51.120
two hours and track where that sunscreen
went

00:02:51.120 --> 00:02:54.319
they didn't use any other products on
their face and they were allowed outside

00:02:54.319 --> 00:02:57.760
for up to an hour
here's a graph of what happened on

00:02:57.760 --> 00:03:02.480
average after two hours there was 16%
less sunscreen, by four hours it dropped

00:03:02.480 --> 00:03:05.840
by another seven percent
and by eight hours it was another four

00:03:05.840 --> 00:03:09.200
and a half percent less
after eight hours there was a total of

00:03:09.200 --> 00:03:11.840
almost thirty percent less dye which
means around

00:03:11.840 --> 00:03:15.840
thirty percent less sunscreen coverage
we can't confidently say that these

00:03:15.840 --> 00:03:19.040
results apply for every sunscreen
because we know that different

00:03:19.040 --> 00:03:21.280
sunscreens will last for different
amounts of time

00:03:21.280 --> 00:03:24.879
so for example if you have a water
resistant sunscreen that tends to last

00:03:24.879 --> 00:03:27.840
better on your skin
if you're using chemical sunscreen

00:03:27.840 --> 00:03:31.519
filters these tend to absorb a little
bit into the top layers of your skin

00:03:31.519 --> 00:03:35.840
and stay around for longer but it means
that there's probably still a

00:03:35.840 --> 00:03:38.159
significant amount of sunscreen left on
your skin

00:03:38.159 --> 00:03:42.640
at the end of the day if you don't move
around that much for most sunscreens

00:03:42.640 --> 00:03:46.080
as an indication if you take 30% away
from spf 50

00:03:46.080 --> 00:03:52.720
you get spf 35 spf 30 becomes spf 21 and
spf 15 becomes spf 10.

00:03:52.720 --> 00:03:57.040
so the higher the spf you start with the
more likely it is that you'll have a

00:03:57.040 --> 00:04:02.000
high amount left at the end of the day
but is this protection enough like i

00:04:02.000 --> 00:04:04.080
said in my video on wearing sunscreen
indoors

00:04:04.080 --> 00:04:08.159
how much protection you need depends on
how much uv you're exposed to

00:04:08.159 --> 00:04:12.319
and how concerned you are about the
effects of uv the main concerns for most

00:04:12.319 --> 00:04:14.920
people
are skin cancer wrinkles and

00:04:14.920 --> 00:04:18.160
hyperpigmentation
so for a lot of people on most days if

00:04:18.160 --> 00:04:20.959
you're not getting a lot of sun and
you're not doing a lot of physical

00:04:20.959 --> 00:04:23.600
activity you're not running around
you're not swimming

00:04:23.600 --> 00:04:27.440
then the amount that you apply in the
morning is probably going to be enough

00:04:27.440 --> 00:04:30.639
but if you're doing a bit more then
you'll probably want to reapply your

00:04:30.639 --> 00:04:34.160
sunscreen
so how do we do it if you're reapplying

00:04:34.160 --> 00:04:37.440
your sunscreen on top of your makeup
then you'll either have to mess up your

00:04:37.440 --> 00:04:41.759
sunscreen or apply less sunscreen or
apply a less even layer

00:04:41.759 --> 00:04:45.280
there isn't really a way to avoid this
compromise unfortunately

00:04:45.280 --> 00:04:48.720
so i've divided my approach to
reapplying sunscreen into three main

00:04:48.720 --> 00:04:52.400
situations that work for me
if i'm not going to get much sun so i go

00:04:52.400 --> 00:04:56.000
to work in the morning i get most of my
indirect exposure in the first two

00:04:56.000 --> 00:04:59.680
hours and then i come home at night in
the dark

00:04:59.680 --> 00:05:03.600
then i don't reapply and i rely on the
sunscreen that i applied in the morning

00:05:03.600 --> 00:05:06.639
to get me through
i also avoid walking in the sun so

00:05:06.639 --> 00:05:09.039
nothing too extreme
i'll walk on the shady side of the

00:05:09.039 --> 00:05:11.680
street i'll sit away from the window on
the train

00:05:11.680 --> 00:05:15.520
this is most days and i found that for
me with my skin type my sun exposure

00:05:15.520 --> 00:05:19.520
situation and the sunscreens i'm using
my freckles are lightening my

00:05:19.520 --> 00:05:23.120
hyperpigmentation goes away quickly
and so i take that as a sign that i'm

00:05:23.120 --> 00:05:26.320
doing enough i've done a video on my
favorite sunscreens which i'll link

00:05:26.320 --> 00:05:28.720
below if you want to find out more about
those

00:05:28.720 --> 00:05:31.840
let's go to the other extreme with lots
and lots of sun

00:05:31.840 --> 00:05:35.280
so let's say i go have breakfast with a
friend in the morning and then we go on

00:05:35.280 --> 00:05:39.199
a four-hour walk around a lot of nice
beaches i'm going to get tons of sun

00:05:39.199 --> 00:05:41.840
exposure i'm probably going to be
sweating a bit

00:05:41.840 --> 00:05:44.960
and i really should be reapplying my
sunscreen every two hours

00:05:44.960 --> 00:05:49.360
because i'll get sunburned in that case
i put on water resistant sunscreen and

00:05:49.360 --> 00:05:53.360
then put on very very simple makeup
so maybe a tinted moisturizer and some

00:05:53.360 --> 00:05:56.479
blush or maybe just concealer in the key
areas

00:05:56.479 --> 00:06:00.240
then every two hours i'll reapply that
water resistant sunscreen and maybe

00:06:00.240 --> 00:06:02.400
touch up the makeup if i feel like i
need it

00:06:02.400 --> 00:06:06.160
i'm not going to bother wearing a lot of
heavy blush or contour or highlighter

00:06:06.160 --> 00:06:09.039
because
i need those to stay in specific areas

00:06:09.039 --> 00:06:12.080
of my face and if i'm reapplying
sunscreen every two hours that's just

00:06:12.080 --> 00:06:14.639
not going to happen
when you're reapplying sunscreen you

00:06:14.639 --> 00:06:16.479
don't have to cleanse properly or
anything

00:06:16.479 --> 00:06:20.639
you can just reapply it on top if you do
have a bit of dirt then you might want

00:06:20.639 --> 00:06:23.039
to wipe it off with a makeup wipe or
something

00:06:23.039 --> 00:06:26.080
so you don't end up just exfoliating
your face while you're putting on

00:06:26.080 --> 00:06:29.199
sunscreen
i'll also wear a hat and sunglasses and

00:06:29.199 --> 00:06:32.400
long-sleeve clothes
and i'll be reapplying sunscreen on my

00:06:32.400 --> 00:06:36.240
body as well then there's the in between
situation which i think is the one that

00:06:36.240 --> 00:06:39.440
most people care about
this is when you want your makeup to

00:06:39.440 --> 00:06:42.960
look pretty good but at the same time
you want to get a decent amount of sun

00:06:42.960 --> 00:06:45.600
protection
say i'm working in the office but i want

00:06:45.600 --> 00:06:49.840
to take a nice long walk at lunch
and i'm going home on the train while

00:06:49.840 --> 00:06:52.560
the sun is coming in horizontally
through the windows

00:06:52.560 --> 00:06:55.599
in this situation i'll have my standard
sunscreen

00:06:55.599 --> 00:06:59.440
and makeup that i've applied in the
morning but i want to reapply sunscreen

00:06:59.440 --> 00:07:02.479
on top of that makeup
the most important thing to remember in

00:07:02.479 --> 00:07:05.599
this situation is that the amount of
protection you get

00:07:05.599 --> 00:07:08.800
scales with the amount of sunscreening
product you apply

00:07:08.800 --> 00:07:12.160
on average this is pretty much linear so
if you apply

00:07:12.160 --> 00:07:15.599
half the amount of product you get half
the amount of protection if you apply a

00:07:15.599 --> 00:07:18.160
quarter
you'll get around a quarter so it's a

00:07:18.160 --> 00:07:22.639
matter of getting as much sunscreen on
your face as possible in an even layer

00:07:22.639 --> 00:07:30.880
while also not disturbing your makeup
that much

00:07:30.880 --> 00:07:34.319
so let's talk about the sorts of
products we can use for reapplication

00:07:34.319 --> 00:07:38.240
first off there probably isn't that much
protection in your foundation

00:07:38.240 --> 00:07:42.240
in a 2006 study they found that on
average people applied around

00:07:42.240 --> 00:07:45.759
0.54 grams of liquid foundation on their
face

00:07:45.759 --> 00:07:48.960
they found that there was a lot of
variation and i

00:07:48.960 --> 00:07:52.240
actually only apply about 0.2 grams of
foundation

00:07:52.240 --> 00:07:55.520
so for that average 0.54 grams of
foundation on

00:07:55.520 --> 00:08:00.000
a 400 square centimeter face you'll get
these actual spfs

00:08:00.000 --> 00:08:03.199
while for 0.2 grams of foundation you'll get
this level of protection

00:08:03.199 --> 00:08:06.800
depending on the labeled spf powders are
even worse

00:08:06.800 --> 00:08:10.879
there was a study that was done in the
80s on 10 000 US women

00:08:10.879 --> 00:08:14.319
and they found that the average
application of face powder was 85

00:08:14.319 --> 00:08:18.000
milligrams
even with an spf 50 powder that gives

00:08:18.000 --> 00:08:21.919
you only
spf 5.5 now there's tons of variation

00:08:21.919 --> 00:08:24.720
with different products and different
people's application

00:08:24.720 --> 00:08:28.800
so i actually emailed a bunch of powder
sunscreen companies to ask if they had

00:08:28.800 --> 00:08:31.919
any data on
the amount of protection you'd expect

00:08:31.919 --> 00:08:36.240
given a typical application of powder
the answers i got back were really

00:08:36.240 --> 00:08:40.080
variable and as much as i want powder
sunscreen to be a thing

00:08:40.080 --> 00:08:44.560
because it's so convenient and so easy i
just didn't really find the data

00:08:44.560 --> 00:08:47.519
convincing
overall it just seems really unlikely

00:08:47.519 --> 00:08:50.880
that you're going to get
enough powder on your face reliably

00:08:50.880 --> 00:08:54.240
every time to get an even continuous
layer that you need for

00:08:54.240 --> 00:08:57.680
any sort of significant protection even
as a top up method

00:08:57.680 --> 00:09:00.720
the most promising data i got was from
colorescience

00:09:00.720 --> 00:09:03.839
their specially treated powder is meant
to stick to skin better

00:09:03.839 --> 00:09:07.519
and it's 80 minutes water resistant
which means that they got people to sit

00:09:07.519 --> 00:09:11.680
in a jacuzzi
for four sessions of 20 minutes with

00:09:11.680 --> 00:09:15.120
that powder on their skin
and at the end they came out and tested

00:09:15.120 --> 00:09:18.320
that patch of skin and they found that
it still had spf 50

00:09:18.320 --> 00:09:22.080
which is really impressive they actually
measured the amount of powder left on

00:09:22.080 --> 00:09:26.399
the skin afterwards and
on average it was 0.542 milligrams per

00:09:26.399 --> 00:09:29.279
square centimeter
this probably doesn't happen with most

00:09:29.279 --> 00:09:32.320
powders that average 85 milligram
application

00:09:32.320 --> 00:09:35.519
is less than half of this so i was
pretty impressed

00:09:35.519 --> 00:09:38.560
i think their sunscreen powder also
works particularly well

00:09:38.560 --> 00:09:42.160
because they have a really high amount
of active ingredients in it

00:09:42.160 --> 00:09:46.880
the bad news is that this result comes
from a standard water resistance test

00:09:46.880 --> 00:09:50.640
and it's pretty unrealistic when it
comes to a powder so they put two

00:09:50.640 --> 00:09:53.440
milligrams per square centimeter of
powder on the skin

00:09:53.440 --> 00:09:57.360
pat it out into an even layer leave it
for 15 minutes and then do the water

00:09:57.360 --> 00:10:00.160
test
even if we assume that all of that extra

00:10:00.160 --> 00:10:03.279
powder that didn't stick on the skin
wasn't necessary

00:10:03.279 --> 00:10:07.760
that 0.542 milligrams per square
centimeter still works out to be 0.22

00:10:07.760 --> 00:10:11.360
grams for your full face
which is quite a lot to get that much

00:10:11.360 --> 00:10:14.959
product out you have to swirl the brush
on your face for a full minute

00:10:14.959 --> 00:10:19.519
while priming the brush four times in
our new standard unit for time that is

00:10:19.519 --> 00:10:22.560
six happy birthdays
which i think is pretty unrealistic

00:10:22.560 --> 00:10:25.839
especially because the instructions
don't tell you specifically how many

00:10:25.839 --> 00:10:29.360
times you need to prime the brush
plus with powder sunscreens a lot of

00:10:29.360 --> 00:10:33.360
them come in really small pack sizes
like seven and a half grams six grams

00:10:33.360 --> 00:10:37.120
even 2.5 grams and so that adds up
really quickly

00:10:37.120 --> 00:10:40.640
i think as far as powders go 
colorescience is probably your best bet

00:10:40.640 --> 00:10:43.760
especially because it's water resistant
and i think this goes for most water

00:10:43.760 --> 00:10:46.480
resistant powders
they are going to be more reliable

00:10:46.480 --> 00:10:48.880
because they go through that water
resistance test

00:10:48.880 --> 00:10:52.320
before their spf is re-measured but i do
think that

00:10:52.320 --> 00:10:55.519
other formats for reapplication are
going to be more reliable

00:10:55.519 --> 00:10:58.800
they're going to be more likely to give
you the protection you want

00:10:58.800 --> 00:11:01.920
so there's only really two options that
i think are reasonable that i've come

00:11:01.920 --> 00:11:05.360
across so far
firstly spray sunscreens these are

00:11:05.360 --> 00:11:08.720
relatively thin sprays that you can
spray over your face

00:11:08.720 --> 00:11:12.399
i think in terms of the evenness of
coverage spray sunscreens

00:11:12.399 --> 00:11:16.399
can perform better than cream sunscreens
that's because they have a really low

00:11:16.399 --> 00:11:19.760
viscosity they're really runny and so
they can get into the valleys of your

00:11:19.760 --> 00:11:23.360
skin better than creams can
this is spraying from 20 centimeters

00:11:23.360 --> 00:11:27.440
away and even though there's droplets i
think if you apply a few layers you can

00:11:27.440 --> 00:11:30.560
get pretty
even coverage again one of the big

00:11:30.560 --> 00:11:35.040
issues is how much you end up applying
it's really easy to underapply if you just

00:11:35.040 --> 00:11:38.240
mist it on casually
i recommend that you do a bunch of thin

00:11:38.240 --> 00:11:42.079
layers and letting it dry in between so
that it doesn't build up and run your

00:11:42.079 --> 00:11:45.200
makeup down your face
you can spray your spray into a quarter

00:11:45.200 --> 00:11:48.640
teaspoon measure to see how many sprays
you need to get a reasonable amount of

00:11:48.640 --> 00:11:51.040
sunscreen
with these three sprays i found that i

00:11:51.040 --> 00:11:55.040
needed between four and nine sprays to
get a quarter teaspoon amount

00:11:55.040 --> 00:11:58.800
i recommend checking this yourself with
your own spray there's also an issue

00:11:58.800 --> 00:12:01.440
with aerosol sunscreens
these are the ones that come in a

00:12:01.440 --> 00:12:04.959
pressurized can and you just hold down
the nozzle and it keeps spraying

00:12:04.959 --> 00:12:08.880
this is actually pretty rare for a face
sunscreen most of them i found aren't

00:12:08.880 --> 00:12:11.839
pumps
a test in choice magazine to which i am

00:12:11.839 --> 00:12:15.360
a proud subscriber
found that for aerosol sunscreens about

00:12:15.360 --> 00:12:18.959
40 to 60%
of it was actually propellant aerosol

00:12:18.959 --> 00:12:22.079
spray sunscreens are tested without the
propellant so that means you would

00:12:22.079 --> 00:12:25.279
actually have to spray for a lot longer
to get the protection you think you're

00:12:25.279 --> 00:12:28.399
getting
a few tips for using spray sunscreens

00:12:28.399 --> 00:12:31.839
try not to inhale it because that's not
good for you

00:12:31.839 --> 00:12:35.279
so i recommend holding your breath
applying it and then walking a couple of

00:12:35.279 --> 00:12:38.320
steps
before breathing again also be really

00:12:38.320 --> 00:12:41.200
careful when you're applying outdoors
because if you just spray it on your

00:12:41.200 --> 00:12:43.200
face
it might blow into your eyes or just

00:12:43.200 --> 00:12:46.320
blow away the other option that i think
is really good

00:12:46.320 --> 00:12:50.160
is if you just reapply your regular
sunscreen lotion over your makeup with a

00:12:50.160 --> 00:12:52.800
cushion puff
james and robert welsh recently did a

00:12:52.800 --> 00:12:55.440
video about reapplying sunscreen over
makeup

00:12:55.440 --> 00:12:59.440
and they recommended cushion compacts as
a good compromise between getting enough

00:12:59.440 --> 00:13:02.240
sunscreen
and not messing up your makeup but one

00:13:02.240 --> 00:13:04.800
of the problems with these
is that most of these products have

00:13:04.800 --> 00:13:07.600
minerals in them and that can give a
white cast

00:13:07.600 --> 00:13:10.880
so what i prefer doing is using the
cushion applicator with

00:13:10.880 --> 00:13:15.120
a regular sunscreen lotion so this
technique has been around in the asian

00:13:15.120 --> 00:13:18.800
skincare community for years
some people actually buy an empty

00:13:18.800 --> 00:13:22.240
cushion container and put the sunscreen
in it so this is what it looks like

00:13:22.240 --> 00:13:26.880
something like this where you've got
like the little applicator on top

00:13:26.880 --> 00:13:30.399
and then underneath you have the
foundation in this case but you can put

00:13:30.399 --> 00:13:33.279
like sunscreen there
i don't think it's necessary though you

00:13:33.279 --> 00:13:35.920
can just use the cushion applicator so
the little

00:13:35.920 --> 00:13:40.480
thing on top and just use your regular
lotion on like the back of your hand

00:13:40.480 --> 00:13:43.839
these applicators are really really
cheap online you shouldn't pay more than

00:13:43.839 --> 00:13:47.199
